# Build-context exclusions for apps/web/Dockerfile and apps/api/Dockerfile.
# Both compose services build with `context: .`, so every path below is
# evaluated once against the repository root.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (BL-17)
# There was no .dockerignore at all, which is harmless on GitHub Actions — a
# fresh `actions/checkout` has no node_modules, no .next, no .env — but not on a
# developer machine, where `docker compose build web` uploads the entire working
# tree (~1.1 GB here: 868 MB node_modules + 200 MB apps/web/.next + 23 MB .git)
# and then bakes host artefacts into the image via the builder stage's
# `COPY apps/web ./apps/web` / `COPY packages ./packages`.
#
# That asymmetry is why a Docker build can fail locally while CI stays green,
# and it got sharper with @sentry/nextjs: it is the first dependency in this
# tree that ships an OS-specific binary package (@sentry/cli-win32-x64 with a
# sentry-cli.exe on Windows vs @sentry/cli-linux-x64 needed inside the image).
# @sentry/cli detects exactly this and aborts with "…installed on one platform
# (for example Windows or MacOS) and then the node_modules folder is reused on
# another operating system (for example Linux in Docker). To fix this, avoid
# copying the node_modules folder" (@sentry/cli/js/helper.js).

# ─── Dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never let a host-installed tree cross into a Linux image. Both Dockerfiles
# install their own via `npm ci` in the deps stage and copy it forward from
# there; anything from the context can only shadow it with foreign binaries.
**/node_modules

# ─── Build outputs regenerated inside the image ───────────────────────────────
# The builder stages run `npm run build -w apps/web` and `npm run build` in
# apps/api, so a stale host copy is dead weight at best and a confusing cache
# hit at worst. NOTE: packages/*/dist is deliberately NOT listed — no Dockerfile
# builds the workspace packages, so their dist must stay available from the
# context.
apps/web/.next
apps/api/dist
**/.turbo

# ─── Local environment files ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# apps/api/.env holds real development secrets (DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, DOKU
# keys) and currently lands in the api builder layer. apps/web/.env.local is
# worse than untidy: Next.js loads it during `next build`, so any NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# the deploy forgot to pass as a build arg is silently filled in from a
# developer's laptop and inlined into the production bundle.
.env
.env.*
apps/*/.env
apps/*/.env.*

# ─── Repository metadata and CI config ────────────────────────────────────────
# Never COPYed by either Dockerfile; excluded purely so the context transfer
# stays small.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.editorconfig

# ─── Test and report artefacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
apps/web/test-results
apps/web/playwright-report
apps/web/blob-report
apps/api/coverage
apps/web/coverage

# ─── Documentation and compose files ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Also never COPYed; kept out of the context for the same reason as .git.
# (apps/web/Dockerfile and apps/api/Dockerfile are deliberately NOT excluded —
# dockerignore patterns are root-relative, not recursive, so a bare `Dockerfile`
# line would be a no-op here and only mislead the next reader.)
docs
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
